---
import RecipeLayout from '../../layouts/RecipeLayout.astro';
---
<RecipeLayout title="Laminated Pastry Base">
  <div class="recipe-title-block">
    <h1>Laminated Pastry Base</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">This one grew up and got a name too.</p>
  </div>

  <main>
    <section class="block">
      <div class="callout">
        <p>The Laminated Pastry Base is the croissant-family foundation behind every laminated bake on this site — a stiff, white-flour-leaning levain (45–50% hydration, 20% of flour weight) softened directly into cold milk before the flour goes in, then given 8–12 hours to peak before the détrempe is built, folded, and turned.</p>
        <p>It's since been given the full recipe treatment, unchanged, and published as <a href="/recipes/build-block-butter/">Build Block Butter</a> — filed under Enriched &amp; Sweet rather than Pastry &amp; Lamination, since a butter block this rich, headed toward sugar-forward pastries, reads as viennoiserie first and technique second. Recipe card, step-by-step tutorial, ingredient history, and a baking playlist all live there. That's the place to go for the complete write-up and the dough this base describes — build it, fold it, freeze it, and it's the starting point for whatever laminated pastry you're in the mood for, croissants included. (Future shape-specific bakes built on this same dough, like a Pain au Chocolat, may land in Pastry &amp; Lamination instead — the section split is by character, not by which recipe the dough itself came from.)</p>
        <p>In the meantime, the site's <a href="/guides/">baking Guides</a> have plenty on the technique behind it — the <a href="/guides/starter/starter-vs-levain/">Starter vs. Levain</a> guide covers why this base uses a stiff starter in the first place, and the <a href="/guides/starter/starter-types-and-flours/">Starter Types and Flours</a> guide has the hydration math behind it.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</RecipeLayout>
